# infra/prod/Caddyfile.example — DIRECT (non-Cloudflare) access to the panel.
#
# Serves the panel on a DNS-only host — an A/AAAA record pointing STRAIGHT to this
# server, NOT proxied by Cloudflare — with automatic Let's Encrypt TLS. Users who
# cannot reach Cloudflare open this host and sign in via the panel's own Google
# login (server-side). Cloudflare Access on the main host keeps working too.
#
# This file is the "port 443 is free" method (INSTALL.md §3.5 method A). If :443 is
# already owned by another service (a site, a VPN edge, an haproxy/nginx SNI
# router), do NOT use auto-HTTPS here: route the host through that terminator to a
# local Caddy on a loopback port and give Caddy a STATIC cert instead, e.g.:
#     { https_port 7443 }
#     direct.example.com {
#         bind 127.0.0.1
#         reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:5430
#         tls /etc/caddy/certs/direct.fullchain.pem /etc/caddy/certs/direct.key.pem
#     }
# (obtain the cert out-of-band, e.g. acme.sh HTTP-01, with a renew+reload hook).
# See INSTALL.md §3.5 method B.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# READ THIS IF THE DOOR EXISTS BECAUSE THE PROXIED HOST IS BEING BLOCKED
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The config at the bottom of this file takes a certificate for ONE name, over
# HTTP-01. That certificate is published in Certificate Transparency, and CT is
# public and searchable — so the exact hostname of your bypass door lands in a
# feed that anyone, including whoever blocked the proxied host, can watch. A door
# built to survive blocking should not announce itself the day it is created.
#
# The alternative is a WILDCARD taken over DNS-01: `*.example.com` discloses only
# `example.com`, which is public anyway. It needs a Caddy with a DNS provider
# module and a token that may edit that zone's DNS:
#
#     caddy add-package github.com/caddy-dns/cloudflare
#     install -m 600 /dev/null /etc/caddy/cf-dns.env
#     # write CF_DNS_API_TOKEN=<token with Zone:DNS:Edit> into it with a HEREDOC,
#     # never `printf` through nested quoting — see the trap note below
#     mkdir -p /etc/systemd/system/caddy.service.d
#     printf '[Service]\nEnvironmentFile=/etc/caddy/cf-dns.env\n' \
#       > /etc/systemd/system/caddy.service.d/override.conf
#     systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl restart caddy
#
# ...with this site block in place of the one below. One block covers the zone, a
# host matcher serves only the door, and everything else is closed rather than
# answered:
#
#     *.example.com {
#         tls {
#             dns cloudflare {env.CF_DNS_API_TOKEN}
#             resolvers 1.1.1.1 8.8.8.8
#         }
#         @panel host direct.example.com
#         handle @panel {
#             encode zstd gzip
#             reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:5430
#         }
#         handle {
#             abort
#         }
#     }
#
# Two traps, both of which cost an hour the first time:
#
#   - `caddy validate` runs OUTSIDE systemd, so it does not read EnvironmentFile
#     and fails with `API token '' appears invalid`. That is not a config error.
#     Validate with `set -a; . /etc/caddy/cf-dns.env; set +a` first.
#   - If the token in that env file picks up a stray character, Cloudflare answers
#     `9109 Invalid access token` on the ACME DNS write while the same token still
#     works fine from `curl` — which sends you hunting for a permissions problem
#     that does not exist. Check the file with `od -c`, not with `cat`.
#
# Setup:
#   1. Add a DNS-only A record, e.g. direct.example.com -> this server's IP
#      (grey cloud in Cloudflare, or any registrar). Port 80/443 must be open.
#   2. Install Caddy, put this file at /etc/caddy/Caddyfile (edit the host below
#      and the email), then: systemctl reload caddy
#   3. In infra/prod/.env set PANEL_PUBLIC_URL=https://direct.example.com plus the
#      GOOGLE_OAUTH_* and PANEL_IDENTITY_SECRET values, and recreate web +
#      control-api. Add https://direct.example.com/api/auth/google/callback as an
#      Authorized redirect URI on the Google OAuth client.

{
	# Let's Encrypt registration email (for expiry notices).
	email admin@example.com
}

direct.example.com {
	encode zstd gzip
	# The panel's web port (loopback, same as the Cloudflare Tunnel targets).
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:5430
}
